{
  "title": "Calorie estimate can't handle counted ingredients (2 large eggs, 1 medium onion, 3 cloves garlic)",
  "description": "In nutrition::grams_per_unit only mass and volume units are supported, so ingredients whose food does match still fail with 'Unsupported quantity unit' when they're counted. \
On the prod corpus (2026-09-27) this is 443 lines, including eggs 134, onion 123, garlic 95, egg 70, and onions 19. \
The units involved are large 190, clove 93, none/bare count 88, medium 46, small 28, plus pinch, head and extra-large.\n\
\n\
These are some of the most common and most calorie-relevant ingredients (eggs especially). \
USDA SR Legacy ships per-food portion weights (food_portion / measure descriptions like '1 large', '1 clove', '1 medium'). \
Extend scripts/import-catalog.py (make catalog-import) to import the relevant per-each portion weights into ramekin-core/src/catalog/data/usda.json (e.g. a portions map per food), then resolve count units (and bare counts, which default to the 'medium' or single-each portion) in grams_per_unit. 'pinch' and 'dash' are trace units: under the step 1 per-line rule (p1-ingredient-catalog-step1-unified-resolver) they make a line negligible only for trace_ok foods (pepper, spices, herbs). \
For any other food (e.g. 'a pinch of sugar') the line is computed if the food has a matching portion weight, and stays unknown otherwise. \
Per-each weights belong on catalog entries, so do this as part of or after step 1.\n\
\n\
Add unit tests in ramekin-core/tests/nutrition_tests.rs for large egg, garlic clove, medium onion and a bare count. \
Bump RULE_VERSION.",
  "status": "open",
  "priority": 2,
  "type": "task",
